 "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a473f950ca02cb6db7ee132f1b9de240125d4e28540344c2d876322ff93788c7"} {"question" "q-336459" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c49b9a56c321999a18632d281dd633429eee145ba4bbc2f3993a28f77ec6916"} {"question" "q-336459" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\cos(2t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c49b9a56c321999a18632d281dd633429eee145ba4bbc2f3993a28f77ec6916"} {"question" "q-336449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}+3t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ second.\n\n$a(\\frac{1}{2})=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c49b9a56c321999a18632d281dd633429eee145ba4bbc2f3993a28f77ec6916"} {"question" "q-336449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}+3t) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}-2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-2t+3) \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ second, we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{1}{2}) & =24{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c49b9a56c321999a18632d281dd633429eee145ba4bbc2f3993a28f77ec6916"} {"question" "q-336449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a473f950ca02cb6db7ee132f1b9de240125d4e28540344c2d876322ff93788c7"} {"question" "q-336449" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c49b9a56c321999a18632d281dd633429eee145ba4bbc2f3993a28f77ec6916"} {"question" "q-336449" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c49b9a56c321999a18632d281dd633429eee145ba4bbc2f3993a28f77ec6916"} {"question" "e-215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = -6t-3t^2$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/example-215.json" "file_sha256" "71b1e773a337012c787a32bdd1bcb66a9a53590aa6d7cb3e9c1d8ab327fa3677"} {"question" "e-215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6-6t \\\\  & =-6(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/example-215.json" "file_sha256" "71b1e773a337012c787a32bdd1bcb66a9a53590aa6d7cb3e9c1d8ab327fa3677"} {"question" "e-216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t) = 1+2t+3t^2+4t^3$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t=0$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/example-216.json" "file_sha256" "92b8ee01c1b36adfbee1b633cbc7fb04a1672a2f309cc1db700837ff6497897e"} {"question" "e-216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t+3{t}^{2}+4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2+6t+12{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+6t+12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6+24t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=0$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =6+24(0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=0$ is $6$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951961/example-216.json" "file_sha256" "92b8ee01c1b36adfbee1b633cbc7fb04a1672a2f309cc1db700837ff6497897e"}] "usage" {"e-216" [] "q-2359" [] "e-215" []} "basis" 777 "retractions" [[17592186088036 :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t) = 4t^{3} + 10t^{2} - 15$, where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t = 3$ seconds."] [17592186087385 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(-6t - 3t^{2}) \\\\\n&=-6 - 6t \\\\\n&=-6(1 + t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087386 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + 2t + 3t^{2} + 4t^{3}) \\\\\n&= 2 + 6t + 12t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2 + 6t + 12t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 + 24t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=0$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(0) &= 6 + 24(0) \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=0$ is $6$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085161 17592186085162 17592186085163 17592186085164 17592186085165] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085160 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085160 :answer-field/id] [17592186085160 :answer-field/key] [17592186085160 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
